================
Sticky MAC Security

Why we did this
================
In Port Security, administrators had to manually configure the MAC address of every device.

This became difficult in large environments because:
- Every PC MAC address had to be collected manually.
- Device replacement required reconfiguration.
- Moves, Adds, and Changes (MAC) were time-consuming.

To reduce administrative effort, Cisco introduced Sticky MAC Security.

Sticky MAC Security automatically learns the MAC address of a device connected to the port and adds it to the running configuration.

What will happen
================
1. A device is connected to the switch port.
2. The switch automatically learns the device MAC address.
3. The MAC address is added to the running configuration.
4. Future connections from the same device are allowed.
5. If another device is connected and maximum MAC limit is reached, a violation occurs.

Example:
PC1 connects to Fa0/1.

MAC Address:
00AA.1111.2222

The switch automatically learns and stores this MAC.

If PC2 is connected later, the switch detects a violation and takes the configured action.

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. No need to manually enter MAC addresses.
2. Easier administration.
3. Faster deployment.
4. Provides the benefits of Port Security with less effort.
5. Good for access-layer ports.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Learns the first device connected, even if it is unauthorized.
2. Does not verify user identity.
3. MAC addresses can still be spoofed.
4. Requires cleanup when devices are permanently replaced.
5. Security is based only on MAC addresses.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Even with Sticky MAC, attackers could spoof a valid MAC address and gain access.

Organizations wanted a way to verify users and devices before granting network access.

This led to:

802.1X Port-Based Authentication

Instead of trusting only a MAC address, the switch authenticates the user/device using credentials through a RADIUS server.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security maximum 1
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security mac-address sticky
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security violation shutdown
Switch(config-if)# end

Verification
============

show port-security
show port-security interface fa0/1
show port-security address
show running-config interface fa0/1

Expected Result
===============

1. Switch automatically learns the first MAC address.
2. MAC address appears in the running configuration.
3. Same device continues to work normally.
4. Any new device causes a security violation.
5. Port enters configured violation state.

Summary
=======

Problem:
Port Security required manual MAC address configuration.

Solution:
Sticky MAC Security automatically learns and saves MAC addresses.

Limitation:
MAC addresses can still be spoofed and users are not authenticated.

Next Practical:
802.1X Port-Based Authentication
